ROUTE 360 – SAFE ROUTE PLANNER
Complete Project Concept and End-to-End Workflow
1. Problem Statement
Normal navigation applications primarily optimize for the shortest or fastest route. However, the fastest route may not always be the safest. Accidents, heavy traffic, flooding, severe weather, road construction, animal intrusion, roadblocks and other hazards can make a route dangerous.
Route 360 addresses this problem by recommending a route based on both travel efficiency and safety.
2. Main Objective
The main objective of Route 360 is to provide intelligent, safety-aware route recommendations using real-time and historical information.
The system answers: “Which route should I take right now?” rather than simply “Which route is shortest?”
3. Core Concept
Route 360 combines user location and destination with information from mapping, traffic, weather and incident sources. The data is processed to estimate the risk of different road segments. Machine learning can then predict risk, while a route-optimization component ranks available routes using safety, travel time and distance.
4. Overall System Architecture
User
  ↓
Web / Mobile UI
  ↓
Node.js + Express Backend
  ↓
Maps Service + Weather API + Traffic Data + Incident Data
  ↓
Data Processing
  ↓
Risk / Safety Engine
  ↓
ML Risk Prediction
  ↓
Route Optimization
  ↓
Ranked Routes
  ↓
Recommended Safe Route
  ↓
User Interface
5. Step 1 – User Input
The user provides:
• Starting location
• Destination
• Travel mode such as car, bike or public transport
• Route preference such as fastest, safest or balanced
The current location can be obtained through GPS/browser geolocation or entered manually.
6. Step 2 – Map Service
A mapping service provides the road network, coordinates, distance, estimated travel time and alternative routes. Route 360 evaluates multiple possible paths instead of considering only one route.
7. Step 3 – Real-Time Data Collection
Route 360 can collect:
• Traffic congestion and travel-time information
• Weather conditions such as rain, wind and visibility
• Accident and incident information
• Flooding and waterlogging information
• Road construction and road closures
• Animal intrusion and other reported hazards
• User-reported incidents such as potholes and roadblocks
8. Step 4 – Data Processing
Raw data from different sources is converted into a common format. Important variables can be transformed into normalized risk values.
Example:
Traffic Risk = 0.8
Weather Risk = 0.7
Accident Risk = 1.0
These values are then used to calculate an overall road or route risk score.
9. Step 5 – Risk Detection
Each road segment can receive a risk score based on several factors.
Example factors:
• Traffic risk
• Accident risk
• Weather risk
• Flood risk
• Road-condition risk
A weighted formula can be used:
Risk Score = 0.25 × Traffic + 0.30 × Accident + 0.20 × Weather + 0.15 × Flood + 0.10 × Road Condition
The weights can later be tuned using historical data.
10. Step 6 – Machine Learning Component
The AI/ML component predicts road or route risk from historical and current conditions.
Possible input features:
• Traffic level
• Rainfall
• Visibility
• Road condition
• Accident count
• Flood probability
• Time of day
• Day of week
• Vehicle type
Possible outputs:
• SAFE
• MODERATE
• HIGH
• CRITICAL
A Random Forest model is a practical starting point because it works well with tabular data, handles nonlinear relationships and is relatively easy to explain.
11. ML Pipeline
Historical Data
  ↓
Data Cleaning
  ↓
Feature Engineering
  ↓
Train/Test Split
  ↓
Model Training
  ↓
Model Evaluation
  ↓
Save Model
  ↓
Backend Integration
  ↓
Real-Time Data
  ↓
Risk Prediction
12. Step 7 – Route Scoring and Optimization
The system should not simply choose the safest route, because an extremely safe route could be unnecessarily long. Instead, Route 360 balances safety with travel time and distance.
Example objective:
Final Score = 0.4 × Travel Time + 0.2 × Distance + 0.4 × Risk
The route with the lowest final score can be recommended. The weights can change according to the user’s preference.
13. User Route Preferences
Fastest:
Time has the highest priority.
Safest:
Safety has the highest priority.
Balanced:
Safety and travel time are given approximately equal importance.
This allows different users to receive different recommendations for the same origin and destination.
14. Frontend Workflow
The interface can contain:
• Start location field
• Destination field
• Travel-mode selection
• Fastest / Balanced / Safest preference
• Find Safe Route button
• Interactive map
• Route cards
• Hazard alerts
• Start Route / Reroute controls
Routes can be visually classified as:
Green = Safe
Yellow = Moderate Risk
Red = High Risk
15. Route Information Card
Each route can display:
• Distance
• Estimated travel time
• Risk level
• Traffic condition
• Weather condition
• Active warnings
• Start Route button
Example:
SAFEST ROUTE
Distance: 12.4 km
ETA: 26 minutes
Risk: LOW
Traffic: Moderate
Weather: Normal
Warning: Minor congestion ahead
16. Hazard Alerts
During travel, Route 360 can warn users when a hazard is detected.
Examples:
• Heavy rainfall ahead
• Accident reported ahead
• Flooded road detected
• Road closure
• High congestion
• Animal intrusion
The system can recommend reducing speed or selecting an alternative route when appropriate.
17. Dynamic Rerouting
Dynamic rerouting is a key feature.
User starts trip
  ↓
Current route is monitored
  ↓
Traffic/weather/incidents are updated
  ↓
Risk is recalculated
  ↓
If the current route becomes unsafe:
  ↓
Alternative routes are calculated
  ↓
Best alternative is selected
  ↓
User receives a rerouting alert
Therefore, Route 360 is continuously adaptive rather than a one-time route calculator.
18. User-Reported Incidents
Users can report hazards such as:
• Accident
• Flood
• Pothole
• Roadblock
• Animal on road
• Heavy traffic
The system records the incident location, type, severity and timestamp. The report can then contribute to warnings and route-risk calculations. A verification mechanism can be added to reduce false reports.
19. Emergency Mode
An emergency mode can prioritize rapid and accessible travel to hospitals, police stations and fire stations. The route engine can give greater importance to travel time and road accessibility while still considering major hazards.
